## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Make every dashboard attention state explainable and actionable. `Needs attention` should identify
the controlled reason, affected project/instance/artifact, age, freshness, and the safe local Tool
Shed route for investigation. Pair it with a change-only "since your last visit" feed covering
work transitions, outcome reconciliation, reporter recovery/staleness, releases, App Server
failures, and enrollment or credential lifecycle events.

### Why It Matters

Counts and colored badges reveal that something may be wrong but do not tell the operator what
happened or what to inspect. The operator must reopen each workspace and rediscover context. A
bounded attention queue and recent-change feed would turn the fleet page into a useful daily review
surface without granting the hosted service control over local work.

### Desired Outcome

An operator can open one attention entry, understand why it exists, see when it began and last
changed, identify its reporting source, follow a stable link to the relevant hosted detail, and see
a suggested local read-only or governed Tool Shed command. Repeated unchanged reports do not create
duplicate attention or history. Resolved conditions leave recent history and disappear from the
active attention queue deterministically.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Use controlled reason and summary codes, stable IDs, timestamps, severity, and source identity;
  do not upload arbitrary explanations, paths, commands, logs, or diagnostics.
- Keep the hosted service advisory and read-only. Suggested routes are text guidance, not remote
  command execution.
- Preserve instance freshness and divergence; one fresh instance must not hide another stale or
  failing instance.
- Retain change events within an explicit bounded window and deduplicate by stable event identity.
- Do not create an email/paging system in the first useful boundary. External notifications remain
  evidence-gated and separate from an accurate in-dashboard attention surface.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Controlled attention-reason records | Explainable, filterable, privacy-bounded, deterministic clearing | Requires contract and hosted model changes | Preferred foundation |
| Render current generic material events | Small UI step | Generic codes lack affected artifact/reason detail | Supplement only |
| Accept reporter-supplied free text | Flexible | Privacy leakage, inconsistent wording, poor grouping | Reject |
| Add external notifications immediately | Proactive | Delivery, recipient, fatigue, and idempotency complexity | Defer until dashboard attention proves useful |

## Open Questions

- Which conditions deserve `attention`, `warning`, or informational recent-history treatment?
- Should "since last visit" be stored per maintainer account, in the browser, or represented only
  by explicit time filters?
- Can suggested local routes be derived entirely server-side from controlled reason codes?
- What acknowledgement semantics, if any, are useful without hiding an unresolved local condition?
- Which enrollment and credential events are useful without exposing security-sensitive detail?

## Decisions

- Keep this separate from `IDEA-0012`: lifecycle/history explains durable work, while this brief
  explains operational attention and recent change.
- Prefer controlled attention reasons and change-only events; reject arbitrary summary text.
- Preserve advisory hosting and route investigation back to governed local Tool Shed commands.
- Defer external notification delivery until accurate attention grouping and clearing are proven.

## Don't Forget

- Attention must say why, not merely repeat the badge color or count.
- A resolved condition should remain discoverable in recent history without remaining active.
- Heartbeats and repeated unchanged reports are not material changes.
- Enrollment approval/rejection/revocation history can be useful, but credentials and identifying
  secrets never belong in the feed.

